course: biology
lesson: 402
title: Wonderland's sea creatures and made-up ones: lobsters, whiting, turtles, the Mock Turtle, the Gryphon and the Caterpillar
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Near the end of «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland», Alice meets a Gryphon and a Mock Turtle, who tell her about their school under the sea and teach her a dance called the Lobster Quadrille, full of lobsters, whiting, porpoises, seals and snails.
explain: Some of these are real sea animals with real habits, some are real animals with a joke stuck on them, and two are not animals at all: the Gryphon comes from old legends and the Mock Turtle comes from a soup.
explain: Mock turtle soup was a real Victorian dish: people who could not afford soup made from real sea turtles made a pretend one from a calf's head. So Carroll invented the creature it is «made from», and the pictures give him a turtle's shell with a calf's head, hooves and tail.
explain: Earlier, Alice nibbles a mushroom to grow and shrink and talks with a hookah-smoking Caterpillar. Real caterpillars do change, but in a different way: they become butterflies or moths. And no real mushroom changes your size, while some wild ones can kill.
fact: Lobsters live on the sea floor, walk on thin legs and have ten legs in all, counting the two big claws; they cannot live long out of water, so a dance on the beach is impossible.
fact: A live lobster is usually dark greenish brown or bluish; it turns red only when it is cooked, which is why the lobster in Alice's poem says «You have baked me too brown».
fact: A whiting is a real silvery fish of the cod family from the North Sea; Victorian cooks served it fried with its tail fixed in its mouth, and that is where the Mock Turtle's «tails in their mouths» joke comes from: live whiting swim like any other fish.
fact: A porpoise is a small sea mammal related to dolphins, with a blunt nose; it breathes air through a blowhole and comes up to the surface to breathe.
fact: Turtles live mostly in water and have flippers or webbed feet; tortoises live on land and have heavy, stumpy legs; the Mock Turtle's teacher is called Tortoise only for the pun «because he taught us».
fact: Sea turtles really do seem to cry: glands near their eyes push out the extra salt from the seawater they swallow, so salty «tears» run down their faces, but not from sadness.
fact: The Mock Turtle is not a real animal: «mock» means pretend, and mock turtle soup was made from a calf's head instead of turtle meat.
fact: A Gryphon, or griffin, is a creature of old legends with the head and wings of an eagle and the body of a lion; it never existed, though some people think ancient fossil skulls found in the desert may have helped the story along.
fact: A caterpillar is the young stage of a butterfly or moth; it eats leaves, grows and sheds its skin several times, then makes a chrysalis or cocoon and comes out with wings.
fact: Alice tells the Caterpillar that one day it will turn into a chrysalis and then a butterfly, which is true science hidden in a silly conversation.
fact: A mushroom is the part of a fungus that pokes above the ground to spread spores; most of the fungus is a web of thin threads under the soil.
fact: Some wild mushrooms, like the death cap, can kill even in small bites, and poisonous ones can look just like safe ones, so you never eat a wild mushroom unless an expert has checked it.
fact: The conger eel, the Mock Turtle's drawing master, is a real long sea fish that hides in rocks and wrecks; the «old crab» who teaches Latin and Greek is a joke about grumpy old teachers.
exercise: Where do real lobsters live? | On the sea floor | On sandy beaches in the sun | In freshwater ponds in the woods | Floating on top of the sea
exercise: How many legs does a lobster have, counting its claws? | Ten | Six | Eight | Twelve
exercise: What colour is a live lobster usually? | Dark greenish brown or bluish | Bright red all over, like a postbox | Pure white with pink spots | Bright yellow with black stripes
exercise: What makes a lobster turn red? | Being cooked | Being angry | Getting sunburnt on the beach | Growing old in the sea
exercise: What is a whiting? | A silvery fish of the cod family | A white bird that lives on the cliffs | A small white sea mammal like a seal | A kind of crab with white shells
exercise: Why did Victorian whiting have their tails in their mouths? | Cooks fixed them that way to fry them | Live whiting swim in circles holding their tails | Whiting bite their tails when they are scared | Fishermen tie them up to stop them escaping
exercise: How does a porpoise breathe? | Through a blowhole, at the surface | Through gills like a fish, under water | Through its skin like a frog | It never needs to breathe
exercise: What is the main difference between turtles and tortoises? | Turtles live mostly in water; tortoises on land | Turtles have shells; tortoises have none | Tortoises are always much smaller than turtles | Tortoises lay eggs and turtles have live babies
exercise: Why do sea turtles look as if they are crying? | Glands near their eyes push out extra salt | They are always sad about the fishing nets | Sand gets into their eyes every day | They cry when they lay their eggs
exercise: What was mock turtle soup made from? | A calf's head | Real sea turtles | Seaweed and fish bones | Old boots and shoes
exercise: What does «mock» mean in «mock turtle»? | Pretend | Very old | Very rare | Sad
exercise: What is a Gryphon? | A legend: eagle in front, lion behind | A real bird that lived in Greece | A large lizard from the islands of Asia | An extinct sea turtle with wings
exercise: What does a caterpillar turn into? | A butterfly or a moth | A worm and then a snake | A beetle with a hard shell | A spider that spins webs
exercise: What does a caterpillar make before it gets its wings? | A chrysalis or cocoon | A nest of twigs in a tree | A burrow deep in the ground | A web like a spider's
exercise: What is a mushroom? | The part of a fungus above ground | A kind of small green plant | The seed of a tree | An animal that does not move
exercise: What should you do with a wild mushroom you find in the woods? | Leave it unless an expert has checked it | Take a small bite to see if it tastes nice | Eat it if it looks like ones from the shop | Cook it, because cooking kills all the poison
test: «the Mock Turtle is a real animal, it's in a book innit» What is true? | No: Carroll made it up from mock turtle soup, which was made from a calf's head | Yes: it is a rare kind of turtle with the head of a calf | Yes: it lived in the cold sea near England until about 1900, when fishermen caught the last one | No: it is just another name for a tortoise
test: Why does the Mock Turtle in the pictures have hooves and a calf's head? | Because the pretend soup was made from a calf's head | Because Carroll did not know what a turtle looks like | Because real turtles have hooves on their back legs | Because he is half cow, like the Gryphon is half lion
test: «found a lobster on the beach, gonna teach it to dance like in Alice lol» What is the real problem? | Lobsters live under water and soon die out of it | Lobsters only dance at night when no one looks | Lobsters need a partner of the same colour | Lobsters can only dance with whiting, not people
test: A lobster in a fish shop is bright red. What can you tell? | It has already been cooked | It is a rare red kind | It is very angry | It is very young
test: A boy says a whiting swims around with its tail in its mouth. What do you tell him? | Live whiting swim normally; the tail-in-mouth was how cooks served them | Yes, that is how all the fish in the cod family swim, so they can steer and stay together | Yes, but only when they are dancing the quadrille | No, whiting have no tails, only fins
test: The Mock Turtle cries all through his story. Which real animal really does have «tears»? | A sea turtle, which gets rid of salt that way | A tortoise, which cries when it is cold | A lobster, which cries when it is cooked | A whiting, which cries out of its gills
test: Is a porpoise a fish? | No, a mammal like a dolphin that breathes air | Yes, a fish with no scales that lives in rivers | Yes, a kind of whiting with a round nose | No, a kind of turtle without a shell
test: Your little sister says the Gryphon is real because she saw one carved on an old building. What is right? | Griffins are legends; people carved them, but none ever lived | They lived in Greece until the Romans hunted them all | They are a kind of eagle still found in the mountains | They were dinosaurs that died out with the others
test: The Caterpillar in the book gets cross when Alice says it will turn into a butterfly one day. Who is right about real caterpillars? | Alice: it will become a chrysalis, then a butterfly or moth | The Caterpillar: caterpillars stay caterpillars all their lives | Neither: caterpillars become worms when they grow up | Alice, but only if it eats a magic mushroom first
test: «Alice ate a mushroom and grew huge, wanna try that with the ones by the path» What would you say? | No: no mushroom changes your size, and wild ones can poison you | Yes, but only eat the right side, like Alice did | Yes, if you cook them first they are always safe | Only if they are the red ones with white spots, because those are the magic ones in all the books
test: What would happen if a whiting tried to breathe on the beach like a porpoise does at the surface? | It could not, because fish take air from water with gills | It would breathe through its blowhole easily | It would grow lungs after a few minutes | It would hold its breath like a turtle
test: ★★★ In the Lobster Quadrille each dancer has a lobster partner. If 6 sea creatures each take one lobster and the Gryphon says, «the lobsters are to be thrown out to sea and you swim after them», how many animals are swimming in the sea at the end, counting the lobsters? | 12 | 6 | 7 | 18
exam: Identify the source from which mock turtle soup was customarily prepared. | The head of a calf, in imitation of genuine turtle soup | The flesh of a rare species of sea turtle | The shells of turtles boiled for many hours | A preparation of seaweed and shellfish
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colour of a living lobster? | It is ordinarily dark greenish brown or bluish, reddening only when cooked | It is invariably red, both when alive upon the sea floor and when cooked for the table | It is white, acquiring colour only from its diet | It is green when young and red in old age
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the creature known as the Gryphon. | It is a legendary beast, part eagle and part lion, which never existed | It is an extinct bird whose bones are displayed in museums | It is a species of lion still found in remote mountains | It is a variety of large eagle native to Greece
exam: Ascertain the correct sequence of development of a butterfly. | Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, adult butterfly | Egg, chrysalis, caterpillar, adult butterfly | Caterpillar, egg, adult butterfly, chrysalis | Egg, adult butterfly, caterpillar, chrysalis
